---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import families from "../data/families.json";
import { byId } from "../lib/people.js";
const f = families.find((x) => x.surname === "Sneyd");
const shown = f.members.filter((m) => !m.living);
---
<Base
  title="The Sneyds"
  eyebrow={`${f.published} published people — the largest family in the archive`}
  dek="Out of Bristol and Kempsey into far western Queensland: married at Muttaburra, born at Boulia — and three of their young men sent to the Great War, of whom the archive knew about one until this week.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Madeley All Saints, read 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The baptism this line rests on, and a burial two years later</h2>

    <p>When the descent from Ralph Sneyd of Keele Hall was disproved, what survived was the baptism
    that disproved it: <strong>William Sneyd, baptised at Madeley All Saints on 12 September 1742,
    father William Sneyd</strong>. Ralph was eighteen that year, so the graft failed — and this
    archive has rested the Sneyd line on that entry ever since.
    <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>The graft →</a></p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The Madeley register has now been read properly. There are 110 Sneyd
    records in it. And two years after that baptism it carries this:</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Event</td><td><strong>Burial</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Date</td><td class="m"><strong>29 September 1744</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Place</td><td>Madeley, All Saints</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Person</td><td><strong>William</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Relationship</td><td><strong>Son of</strong> William SNEYD</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>If that is the same child, this line is anchored to a boy who died at two.</strong>
      The burial gives no age, so it is not certain — but a William son of William baptised in
      September 1742 and a William son of William buried in September 1744, in one small parish, is
      the obvious reading, and no other William son of a William appears in the register in that
      generation. <span class="chip inf">inferred, and the archive cannot yet rule it out</span>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>It cannot be settled from the register.</strong> FamilySearch carries this man as
      <em>William Snead II, 1742–1827</em>, which would require a burial at Madeley in 1827 — and
      Madeley's transcription appears to stop around 1812, so that cannot be tested here either way.
      <span class="chip sup">a coverage limit, not a null</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Corrected the same evening, 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Two of the four links are documented, and I said none were</h2>

    <p class="lede">Earlier today this page said the English origin of these Sneyds was
    "<em>not currently evidenced by anything</em>". <strong>That was an overstatement and it was
    mine.</strong> I searched for two baptisms, did not find them, and stopped — without following up
    a marriage sitting in the same results list, or trying the parish the family actually lived in.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What the tree claims</th><th>What the registers hold</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>William Sneyd married Mary Blackbourne</strong><div class="small">the parents of Samuel, born 1769</div></td>
            <td><strong>Found.</strong> Madeley All Saints, <strong>27 March 1769</strong>, entry 68.
              William Sneyd, <strong>weaver</strong>, and Mary BLACKBOURNE. Witnesses
              <strong>Robert Sneyd</strong> and John Kidd.
              <span class="chip doc">documented</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>William Sneyd baptised 1746, Madeley</strong></td>
            <td>Not there. Madeley holds Samuel 1745, Joseph 1746, John 1748, Simeon 1751 and no
              William, in a parish transcribed continuously.
              <span class="chip lore">searched and empty</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Samuel Sneyd baptised 1769, Madeley</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Found — in 1781.</strong> Madeley baptises a Samuel, son of William, on
              <strong>4 June 1781</strong>, within a family of thirteen children born to William
              Sneyd the weaver between 1769 and 1794. The year 1769 belongs to his eldest brother
              William. <a href={u("/sneyds")}>The family →</a>
              <span class="chip doc">documented, and the tree's year corrected</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Samuel Charles Sneyd, born Hanley 1810, son of Samuel and Elizabeth</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Found.</strong> Hanley, St John the Evangelist — <strong>born 13 March 1811,
              baptised 28 April 1811</strong>, father <strong>Samuel</strong>, mother
              <strong>Elizabeth</strong>, abode Hanley.
              <span class="chip doc">documented</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Two of the four links are written down, and the two that are missing are baptisms
      between them.</strong> That is an ordinary evidential position — better than the Saniger line
      and far better than what this page said at lunchtime.
      <a href={u("/what-we-got-wrong")}>Recorded as an error →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The parish nobody had tried</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Hanley, and a servant's family in the Potteries</h2>

    <p>Every previous search had gone to <strong>Madeley</strong>, because that is where the
    disproved Keele graft pointed. <strong>Hanley had never been searched</strong> — and Hanley is
    where the tree plainly says Samuel Charles was born.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem">It holds his baptism, and a sister: <strong>Mary Sneyd, baptised
    18 February 1814</strong>, the same father Samuel and mother Elizabeth, and this time the clerk
    recorded the father's occupation — <strong>Servant</strong>.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Which makes a line of it.</strong> A <em>weaver</em> marrying at Madeley in 1769; his
      son a <em>servant</em> at Hanley in the 1810s; and that man's son enlisting in the 4th
      (King's Own) and ending as <a href={u("/sergeant-sneyd")}>Governor of Brisbane Gaol</a>. Three
      generations out of the Staffordshire Potteries, and not one of them within reach of Keele Hall.
      <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>The graft that claimed otherwise →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>And a correction to the family tree.</strong>
    The tree gives Samuel Charles's birth as <strong>15 March 1810</strong>. The Bishop's Transcript
    records the birth date outright as <strong>13 March 1811</strong> — two days and one year out.
    The archive publishes the register's date. <span class="chip doc">registered</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Ivy Miriam Sneyd married Lindesay Atkinson D'Arcy at Albion Church in March 1916.
    Her family is the biggest in this archive — <strong>{f.total}</strong> people in the tree — and
    the one that pushed furthest into the Queensland interior.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Muttaburra, 1891</h3>
        <p><strong>Arthur William Hartley Sneyd</strong> (b. Brisbane 1866) married
        <strong>Martha Blum</strong> (b. Rockhampton 1870) at <strong>Muttaburra</strong> on
        20 January 1891 — six hundred kilometres inland, in the central-west sheep country.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Boulia, 1891</h3>
        <p><strong>Ivy Miriam Sneyd</strong> was born at <strong>Boulia</strong> on 31 October 1891,
        nine months after that wedding and another four hundred kilometres further west, near the
        Northern Territory border. It is the remotest birthplace in the archive by a long way.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">20 August 1916</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Arthur Hartley Sneyd, killed at Pozières</h2>
    <p>Ivy's younger brother, born at Rockhampton on 11 October 1893, schooled at Eagle Junction State
    School, and a <strong>typist</strong> by trade — his own attestation paper says so, where this page
    used to say "clerk". He was <strong>killed in action on 20 August 1916</strong>, aged twenty-two.
    <strong>He has no grave.</strong> This page used to say he was "buried in France"; the report of
    death gives his burial place as "<em>Not yet to hand</em>", and it never came to hand.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">Five months earlier his sister had married Lindesay Atkinson D'Arcy at
    Albion; three months earlier Lindesay had sailed for the war from Sydney aboard HMAT
    <em>Demosthenes</em>. Lindesay came home. Arthur did not.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">He was <strong>Private 5050</strong> of the
    <strong>9th Infantry Battalion</strong>, 16th reinforcements, who sailed on HMAT
    <em>Star of Victoria</em> A16 on 31 March 1916. He had been made a sergeant in Australia and
    <strong>gave up the rank to get to France</strong>. He joined the battalion in the line on
    29 July 1916 and was killed twenty-two days later. He has <strong>no known grave</strong>.
    <a href={u("/great-war")}>His service record →</a></p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And he was not the only one.</strong> The Sneyds sent three young men to that war and
      the archive knew about one of them until 9 September 2026.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Vivian Claude Sneyd</strong> (1895–1949), Arthur's younger brother, enlisted two months
      <em>before</em> him and served as a stretcher-bearer with the <strong>3rd Australian Field
      Ambulance</strong>. He came home with the <strong>Military Medal and Bar</strong>. This archive
      had four words about him: "<em>Salesman, listed as occupation on a death notice</em>."
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Ernest Vivian William Sneyd</strong> (1890–1919), their first cousin — son of
      <a href={u("/people/thomas-george-sneyd")}>Thomas George Sneyd</a> — was a labourer once turned
      away for being too short. He got in on a second try, was wounded with the 31st Battalion at the
      end of September 1917, and came home on a hospital ship in January 1919 with his kidneys
      failing. He was dead within the year.
      <br /><br />
      <a href={u("/great-war")}><strong>All four men, and their records →</strong></a> ·
      <a href={u("/finding-arthur")}>The search for Arthur →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Tested, 9 September 2026 — and broken</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The Sneyds were not of Keele Hall</h2>
    <p>This archive flagged the risk and then acted on it. The tree ran this family back to
    <strong>Ralph Sneyd, Esq., of Keele Hall</strong> (1723–1793) and on into Warwickshire gentry of
    the 1530s — eleven generations. <strong>The join is false.</strong> The Madeley baptism of 1742
    names the child's father as <em>William</em> Sneyd, not Ralph; Ralph was eighteen in 1742; and
    Ralph's own recorded children, baptised at Keele and Stone by a wife named <strong>Barbara</strong>,
    include no William at all. <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>The evidence, in full →</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">What the family actually is turns out to be better: Sneyds — often
    written <em>Snead</em> — of <strong>Madeley</strong>, who produced a <strong>grocer at
    Hanley</strong>, whose son <strong>Samuel Charles Sneyd</strong> came out to New South Wales in
    1832 with the <strong>4th (King's Own) Regiment</strong>, shot a bushranger dead near the Vale of Clwydd as a
    sergeant of Mounted Police in 1839, and ended as <strong>Governor of Brisbane Gaol</strong>.
    <a href={u("/sergeant-sneyd")}>His story →</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">The Australian generations are solid. <strong>William Hartley
    Sneyd</strong> was born at Kempsey, New South Wales, on <strong>30 September 1837</strong> — his
    Queensland death registration (<span class="mono">1902/B/2776</span>) records the date outright —
    died 11 September 1902, nineteen days short of his sixty-fifth birthday, and was buried at
    <strong>Lutwyche Cemetery, Kedron</strong>, where his wife Miriam Wakefield and his daughter-in-law
    Martha Blum lie too. <a href={u("/inquest-1902")}>This archive briefly published 1838 and was
    wrong →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What replaced it</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The real line, out of the Madeley registers</h2>
    <p>Taking away Keele Hall did not leave a hole. The parish registers of
    <strong>All Saints, Madeley</strong> carry the family's own line, and it reaches nearly as far
    back in time as the false one did at its lower end:</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th class="m">Record</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>William Sneyd</strong> the elder</td><td class="m">fl. 1720s–1740s</td>
              <td>Madeley. A son <em>John</em> baptised 1724, father "Wm Sneyd"; a son
              <em>William</em> baptised 1742</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>William Sneyd</strong></td><td class="m">bap. 1742 · bur. 13 May 1827</td>
              <td>Baptised All Saints, Madeley, father William. <strong>Married Mary Blackbourne at
              Madeley, 27 March 1769</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Samuel Sneyd</strong>, grocer</td><td class="m">bap. 4 Jun 1781 · bur. 1855</td>
              <td>Madeley, father William. Married Elizabeth Margaret Oliver at Audlem, Cheshire,
              22 October 1809. Died at No. 3 Well Street, <strong>Hanley</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Samuel Charles Sneyd</strong></td><td class="m">b. 15 Mar 1810 · d. 4 Jul 1885</td>
              <td>Hanley → New South Wales with the 4th (King's Own), 1832 →
              <a href={u("/sergeant-sneyd")}>sergeant of Mounted Police</a> → Brisbane</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>William Hartley Sneyd</strong></td><td class="m">b. 30 Sep 1837 · d. 11 Sep 1902</td>
              <td>Kempsey, New South Wales. Buried Lutwyche</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">The 1769 marriage is the keystone: the tree named
    <strong>Mary Blackbourne</strong> as this William's wife and it was right, but carried no source.
    The Madeley register has her, with banns, on 27 March 1769. Their children were baptised there
    from 1776 to 1794 — Sarah, James, <strong>Samuel</strong>, Lydia, Mary, Ann, Fletcher and
    Margaret.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>A warning for anyone working these registers.</strong> There was more than one Sneyd
      household at Madeley at once. A <em>second</em> William Sneyd married <strong>Ann Dissington</strong>
      there on 4 October 1779, and his children are interleaved with the first William's in the same
      register — which is why the baptisms show a William in November 1780 and a Samuel in June 1781,
      seven months apart and therefore not brothers. The archive has not tried to split every entry
      between the two families, and says so rather than guessing.
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">One correction this produced: the tree gives Samuel the
    grocer as <strong>born 1769</strong>, which is where its absurd twelve-year gap between birth and
    christening came from. He was baptised in <strong>1781</strong>; the 1851 census gives his birth
    as about 1779 at Madeley; he was buried at Hanley in 1855, not 1856.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Queensland State Archives, read 9 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The inquest of 1902</h2>
    <p><strong>William Hartley Sneyd died on 11 September 1902, and a coroner investigated
    it.</strong> The family tree records a date and nothing else. Queensland State Archives holds the
    file, it has been downloaded and read in full, and it is published here:</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Item</td><td class="mono">ITM2736504</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Subject</td><td><strong>Inquest file</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Series</td><td>S36 — Coronial Files (Inquests and No Inquests)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Previous IDs</td><td class="mono">JUS/N309 · SRS36/5/308</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Digital copy</td><td><strong>DR103140 · PDF · 15.24 MB · open, no restrictions</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">He was found in <strong>Victoria Park</strong>, Brisbane, on the
    morning of 12 September, between two stacks of railway rails about two hundred yards in from
    Gregory Terrace. The Government Analyst found an ounce and a quarter of crude carbolic acid in his
    stomach, against a fatal dose of an eighth of an ounce. The magisterial inquiry entered the cause
    of death as <strong>"Poisoning (suicide)"</strong>. He was sixty-four, had had asthma for a dozen
    years, and had not been able to work for a dozen years after an injury to his arm.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>His widow's deposition settles the line.</strong> Asked for the particulars, Miriam
      Sneyd swore that "<em>his father's name was Samuel Sneyd, Gaol Governor, his mother's name was
      Catherine</em>" — confirming, from inside the family and under oath, the join to
      <a href={u("/sergeant-sneyd")}>Samuel Charles Sneyd</a> that this archive had been testing. Her
      evidence also names all six
      children with ages that match the tree exactly.
      <br /><br />
      <a href={u("/inquest-1902")}><strong>The whole file, transcribed →</strong></a>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">The same search turned up an <strong>intestacy file</strong> for
    <strong>Arthur Hartley Sneyd</strong> — the man killed at Pozières — opened by the Public Curator
    in 1916 and closed in 1917; land dealings for <strong>William Hartley Sneyd</strong> from 1863 and
    1864; a selection file for <strong>Joseph Samuel Sneyd</strong> at Bundaberg; and a
    <em>"dead farm file"</em> for <strong>Arthur William Hartley Sneyd</strong> at <strong>Aramac</strong>,
    1892–95 — which is the missing middle of the western Queensland decade in which he married at
    Muttaburra and his daughter Ivy was born at Boulia.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>The whole QSA index, re-read on 9 September 2026.</strong> Thirty-three items carry the
      name. Two of them are <strong>Queensland Police staff files</strong> — series
      <span class="mono">S1108</span>, <em>SNEYD, Hanley</em> (AF1623, 1881–83) and
      <em>SNEYD, Samuel Charles</em> (AF1599, 1890–97). Neither can be the gaol governor: he died in
      <strong>1885</strong>, before both date ranges. On the balance of dates they are two of his sons
      — <strong>Samuel Hanley Hanley Stafford Sneyd</strong> (1851–1901) and
      <strong>Samuel Charles Sneyd the younger</strong> (1863–1938).
      <span class="chip inf">identification inferred from dates alone</span>
      <br /><br />
      So the family put at least two more men into the Queensland Police after the father — and the
      father's own service record is still not found. <a href={u("/sergeant-sneyd")}>His story →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <strong>And William Hartley Sneyd went insolvent in 1873.</strong>
      <span class="mono">ITM1056950</span> — <em>Insolvency Files, Public Curator Office, Brisbane</em>,
      file <strong>230/1873</strong>, 6 October to 3 November 1873. He was thirty-six, a contractor,
      and this archive did not know. It is not digitised.
      <br /><br />
      Set it beside what his widow swore in 1902 — that he "<em>had not been able to work for about
      12 years in consequence of an injury to his arm</em>" — and the shape of a working life emerges
      that the family tree recorded as two dates. <a href={u("/inquest-1902")}>The inquest →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>The people</h2>
    <div class="grid three" style="margin-top:1rem">
      {shown.map((m) => {
        const o = byId.get(m.id);
        return (
          <a class="cardlink" href={u(`/people/${o ? o.slug : ""}`)}>
            <div class="nm">{m.name}</div>
            <p class="small" style="margin-top:.3rem">{m.life || "dates unknown"}
            {m.bornPlace ? ` · ${m.bornPlace.split(",")[0]}` : ""}</p>
          </a>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
